24. Overall ER relationship
Logically our system looks like:

                 AUTH DB
                   │
            ┌──────┴──────┐
            │             │
          USER           ROLE
            │
            │
            ▼
      ┌─────────────┐
      │   LOTTERY   │
      └──────┬──────┘
             │
       ┌─────┴──────┐
       │            │
       ▼            ▼
 PRIZE_TIER    LOTTERY_RULE
       │
       │
       ▼
     TICKET
       │
       │
       ▼
     DRAW
       │
       ▼
    WINNER
       │
       ▼
  PRIZE_CLAIM
       │
       ▼
  SETTLEMENT
But physically these tables live in different databases.

25. Why we don't use cross-database foreign keys
Suppose:

ticket_db.tickets.lottery_id
references:

lottery_db.lotteries.id
That creates tight coupling.

Instead:

ticket_db
tickets
-----------------
lottery_id = 1001
The Ticket Service can ask:

GET /api/lotteries/1001
when it needs lottery information.

This gives us independent services.

26. Indexing strategy
We're not going to blindly put indexes on every column.

Important indexes include:

lottery_code
lottery status
lottery dates

ticket_id
lottery_id
customer_id
agent_id
ticket status

draw_id
lottery_id
draw status

winner_id
ticket_id
customer_id

audit resource
audit user
audit timestamp
Indexes improve reads but make writes more expensive and consume storage.

27. Database migrations
Don't manually execute these SQL scripts every time.

We'll use:

Flyway

Our service will eventually look like:

lottery-service/
│
├── src/
│   └── main/
│       ├── java/
│       └── resources/
│           ├── application.yml
│           └── db/
│               └── migration/
│                   ├── V1__create_lotteries.sql
│                   ├── V2__create_prize_tiers.sql
│                   ├── V3__create_lottery_rules.sql
│                   └── V4__create_schedules.sql
Flyway maintains the migration history.

28. One important improvement
I don't want us to rush into creating JPA entities for every table yet.

First we'll define the API contracts and domain boundaries.

Otherwise we can end up with anemic entities and services that simply do:

Controller
    ↓
Service
    ↓
Repository
    ↓
Database
without real business logic.

Our goal is:

HTTP
 ↓
Controller
 ↓
Application Service
 ↓
Domain/business rules
 ↓
Repository
 ↓
Database
