Yes. For your Lottery Management System, I actually recommend moving to multiple databases now if your goal is to learn a proper microservices architecture.

Instead of one lottery_db, we can create one MySQL database per service, while keeping the same table ownership.

Proposed database structure
Database	Service	Tables
lottery_auth_db	Auth Service	users, roles, user_roles
lottery_db	Lottery Service	lotteries, prize_tiers, lottery_rules
lottery_ticket_db	Ticket Service	tickets, ticket_transactions
lottery_draw_db	Draw Service	draws, winning_numbers
lottery_winner_db	Winner Service	winners, prize_claims
lottery_audit_db	Audit Service	audit_events
This matches the database-per-service architecture from your project design. 

Important change
We should not use foreign keys across databases/services.

For example:

lottery_ticket_db.tickets
        |
        | lottery_id
        ↓
lottery_db.lotteries
lottery_id is just a value in the Ticket Service. The Ticket Service gets lottery information through the Lottery Service API, not through a cross-database FK.

Similarly:

ticket_db
    ticket_id
       ↓
winner_db
    winners
The Winner Service communicates with Ticket/Draw services through REST/Kafka rather than directly accessing their tables.

