Step 4 — Understand the User.java entity
1. What is an Entity?
An entity is a Java class that represents a database table.

In our project:

Java class: User.java

Represents one user in Java

JPA / Hibernate mapping

MySQL table: users

Stores user records permanently

For example, a record in MySQL might look like this:

id

username

email

status

1

admin

admin@example.com

ACTIVE

2

customer1

customer1@example.com

ACTIVE

Each database row represents one User object.

2. Package declaration and imports
package com.lottery.auth.entity;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;

package com.lottery.auth.entity;
A package organizes related Java classes.

com.lottery — your application.

auth — the authentication microservice.

entity — classes representing database entities.

This organization becomes useful when the project grows to include controllers, services, repositories, and configuration.

import jakarta.persistence.*;
This imports JPA annotations, including:

@Entity

@Table

@Id

@GeneratedValue

@Column

@PrePersist

@PreUpdate

What is JPA?

JPA stands for Java Persistence API. It defines how Java objects map to relational database tables. Hibernate is the JPA implementation commonly used by Spring Boot.

The * means all types in that package are available by their simple names. It doesn't import every Java class.

import java.time.LocalDateTime;
LocalDateTime represents a date and time without a time-zone offset.

For example:

LocalDateTime now = LocalDateTime.now();

It might represent 2026-10-09T16:30:00, depending on your system clock.

For production applications, we should standardize how timestamps and time zones are handled. For this first entity, we'll keep the mapping simple.

3. Declare the entity
@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {

Let's break it down.

@Entity
Tells Hibernate that this class should be managed as a persistent database entity.

Without @Entity, Hibernate won't treat User as a normal JPA entity.

@Table(name = "users")
Specifies the database table to which the entity maps.

Our mapping is:

Java class: User
       ↓
MySQL table: users
The Java class uses the singular name User; the database table uses the plural name users.

public class User
Defines a public Java class named User.

We can create objects like this:

User user = new User();
user.setUsername("customer1");
user.setEmail("customer1@example.com");

At this point, the object exists only in Java memory. It isn't automatically saved to MySQL.

4. The primary key
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
private Long id;

@Id
Marks id as the entity's primary key.

A primary key uniquely identifies each database row.

@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
Tells Hibernate to rely on the database's identity-generation mechanism. In our MySQL schema, that is:

id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY

When a new user is inserted, MySQL generates the ID.

For example:

First user  → id = 1
Second user → id = 2
Third user  → id = 3
Don't manually assign these IDs during normal registration.

private Long id;
Long represents a 64-bit integer.

private restricts direct access from outside the class.

The ID is nullable before the database inserts the new user.

We use the wrapper type Long rather than primitive long because a new entity can have a null ID.

5. Username and email
@Column(nullable = false, unique = true, length = 100)
private String username;

@Column(nullable = false, unique = true, length = 255)
private String email;

@Column
Controls how a Java field maps to a database column.

For username:

nullable = false means the database column must not contain NULL.

unique = true means duplicate usernames are not allowed.

length = 100 sets the column length to 100 characters.

For email, the maximum length is 255.

The corresponding database definition is approximately:

username VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL UNIQUE,
email VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE

Important: Database constraints are essential because two users might try to register the same username simultaneously. Application validation alone cannot guarantee uniqueness.

We'll also validate requests at the API layer later.

private String username;
String stores text.

We use private fields to protect the object's internal state and expose controlled access through methods.

6. Password storage
@Column(name = "password_hash", nullable = false, length = 255)
private String passwordHash;

There are two names here:

Java field: passwordHash

Database column: password_hash

The name attribute explicitly maps one to the other.

Why store a hash instead of the original password?

Suppose a user registers with a password. Our future registration service will use BCrypt to produce a password hash, then store that hash in this field.

Conceptually:

User enters password
        ↓
BCrypt hashes password
        ↓
password_hash column stores hash
During login, Spring Security's password encoder compares the submitted password with the stored hash.

Never store plain-text passwords. Never return passwordHash in an API response.

7. First name, last name, and phone
@Column(name = "first_name", length = 100)
private String firstName;

@Column(name = "last_name", length = 100)
private String lastName;

@Column(length = 20)
private String phone;

These fields store optional profile information.

The name attribute maps firstName to first_name and lastName to last_name.

The columns are nullable because we haven't marked them nullable = false.

We store phone numbers as String, not Long, because phone numbers aren't quantities used in arithmetic. A string can preserve leading zeros and a + country prefix.

8. User status
@Column(nullable = false, length = 30)
private String status = "ACTIVE";

This field represents whether an account is active.

For our first version, we'll use values such as:

ACTIVE

INACTIVE

LOCKED

The initial Java value is "ACTIVE".

However, that Java default is not a complete security policy. In a production registration flow, account activation and locking must be handled explicitly, and we should validate permitted status values.

The MySQL schema also has a default of ACTIVE. We'll keep the database and application defaults consistent.

9. Created and updated timestamps
@Column(name = "created_at", nullable = false, updatable = false)
private LocalDateTime createdAt;

@Column(name = "updated_at", nullable = false)
private LocalDateTime updatedAt;

These fields track when a user record was created and last updated.

createdAt
updatable = false tells Hibernate not to include this column in normal entity update statements.

It helps preserve the original creation time.

updatedAt
This timestamp should change whenever the user entity is updated through the relevant JPA lifecycle callbacks.

The MySQL schema also defines automatic timestamp defaults and updates. We'll need to keep these responsibilities consistent so that the Java callbacks and database behavior don't unexpectedly disagree.

10. @PrePersist
@PrePersist
protected void onCreate() {
    LocalDateTime now = LocalDateTime.now();
    createdAt = now;
    updatedAt = now;
}

@PrePersist is a JPA lifecycle callback.

JPA invokes this method before inserting a new entity into the database.

The line:

LocalDateTime now = LocalDateTime.now();

gets the current date and time from the system clock.

Both timestamps receive the same value because creation and last-update time are initially identical.

For example:

created_at = 2026-10-09 16:30:00
updated_at = 2026-10-09 16:30:00
11. @PreUpdate
@PreUpdate
protected void onUpdate() {
    updatedAt = LocalDateTime.now();
}

JPA calls this method before updating an existing managed entity.

The creation timestamp stays unchanged, while the update timestamp receives a new value.

Note that JPA lifecycle callbacks apply to normal entity persistence operations. Bulk JPQL updates and direct SQL updates can bypass them, so those operations need separate consideration.

12. Constructor
public User() {
}

This is a no-argument constructor.

JPA requires an entity to have a public or protected no-argument constructor so it can instantiate the entity when loading records.

We can also use it when creating a new object:

User user = new User();

The constructor doesn't save the object to the database.

13. Getters and setters
Example:

public String getUsername() {
    return username;
}

public void setUsername(String username) {
    this.username = username;
}

A getter reads a field's value.

String name = user.getUsername();

A setter changes a field's value.

user.setUsername("customer1");

The keyword this refers to the current object.

In:

this.username = username;

this.username is the field belonging to the current User object.

username is the value passed into the method.

We provide getters for the ID and timestamps but no setters for them. That prevents ordinary calling code from changing these fields directly through setters.

For a larger application, we can use Lombok to reduce boilerplate, but explicit getters and setters are helpful while learning.

14. What happens when we save a user?
Once we build the registration service, the flow will be:

Registration API

Receives username, email and password

Validation and BCrypt

Validates input and hashes the password

UserRepository

Calls the JPA persistence layer

MySQL: lottery_auth_db.users

Stores the new record

The entity defines the mapping. The repository performs database operations, and the service will contain registration logic.

