5.3 Understand every part
A. Package declaration
package com.lottery.auth.repository;

This puts the class in the repository package. We keep database-access code separate from business logic.

B. Import User
import com.lottery.auth.entity.User;

Our repository works with the User entity we created in Step 4. That entity maps to the MySQL users table.

C. Import JpaRepository
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;

JpaRepository is a Spring Data JPA interface that provides common database operations.

For example, it already gives us methods such as:

Method

Purpose

save(user)

Insert or update a user

findById(id)

Find a user by primary key

findAll()

Retrieve all users

deleteById(id)

Delete a user by ID

count()

Count records

We don't need to write SQL for these basic operations.

D. Why is it an interface?
public interface UserRepository
        extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {

An interface defines operations without implementing all of them itself.

Spring Data JPA generates the repository implementation at runtime and provides it to Spring's dependency injection system.

The two generic types mean:

User — the entity this repository manages.

Long — the Java type of the entity's primary key (User.id).

E. Find a user by username
Optional<User> findByUsername(String username);

Spring Data JPA interprets the method name and generates the appropriate query.

Conceptually, it performs:

SELECT *
FROM users
WHERE username = ?;

Why use Optional<User>?

A username might not exist. Optional represents either a result or no result without requiring the method to return null.

Example:

Optional<User> user =
        userRepository.findByUsername("customer1");

if (user.isPresent()) {
    System.out.println(user.get().getEmail());
}

We'll use this method during login to find the account by username.

F. Find a user by email
Optional<User> findByEmail(String email);

This works similarly, but searches the email field.

We'll use it to check whether an email is already registered.

G. Check username existence
boolean existsByUsername(String username);

Returns true if a matching user exists, otherwise false.

Example:

if (userRepository.existsByUsername("customer1")) {
    System.out.println("Username already exists");
}

This is useful for registration validation.

H. Check email existence
boolean existsByEmail(String email);

This performs the equivalent existence check for email addresses.

Important: These checks improve user feedback, but database UNIQUE constraints must remain in place. Two simultaneous requests can both pass an existence check, so registration must also handle duplicate-key errors safely.

